        ":question/worked-solution": "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by the limit\n\n$$\nf_y(1,1)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(1, 1+h)-f(1,1)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y)=-2{y}^{2}x,$ we have that\n\n$$\nf(1, 1+h)=-2(1+h)^{2}(1),\\qquad f(1,1)=-2(1)^{2}(1).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf_y(1,1)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{(-2(1+h)^{2}(1))-(-2(1)^{2}(1))}{h}).\n$$\n\nHere $h\\ne 0$ before taking the limit, and $x=1$ is held constant."
